body:
Two additions after digging further.

**Precedent for this bug class:** #4806 was the mirror image of this report (`parseBody()` first, then `text()`/`json()` breaking through the body cache) and was confirmed as a bug and fixed in #4807. So cross-helper reads through `bodyCache` are intended to work, which supports treating this as a regression rather than unsupported usage.

**Related (but pre-existing, not part of the 4.12.28 regression): `cloneRawRequest` has the same boundary mismatch for multipart.** When the body was already consumed and cached as `formData`, `cloneRawRequest` rebuilds the request as `new Request(url, { body: await req.formData(), headers: req.header() })`. The explicit original `Content-Type` header (old boundary) wins over the one the new FormData serialization would set, so the cloned request's header boundary doesn't match its body bytes:

```ts
import { Hono } from "hono";
import { cloneRawRequest } from "hono/request";

const app = new Hono();
app.post("/x", async (c) => {
  await c.req.formData(); // consumed + cached
  const cloned = await cloneRawRequest(c.req);
  const headerCt = cloned.headers.get("content-type");
  const bodyFirstLine = (await cloned.text()).split("\r\n")[0];
  return c.json({ headerCt, bodyFirstLine });
});

const fd = new FormData();
fd.append("file", new Blob(["hello"], { type: "text/plain" }), "t.txt");
const res = await app.request("/x", { method: "POST", body: fd });
console.log(await res.json());
// header content-type : multipart/form-data; boundary=----formdata-undici-099541084887
// body first line     : ------formdata-undici-076541706477  <-- different boundary
```

Calling `cloned.formData()` accordingly rejects with `Failed to parse body as FormData.`. This one reproduces on 4.12.27 as well as 4.12.30 (controls pass: unconsumed body via `raw.clone()`, and text/json bodies), so it's a separate long-standing defect sharing the same root pattern: re-serializing a cached `FormData` while keeping the original `Content-Type` header. A fix that reuses the cached `FormData` directly (or regenerates the header from the re-serialized body) would likely address both.

Happy to split the `cloneRawRequest` part into its own issue if you prefer.
